---
import PageHero from '@src/components/pages/PageHero.astro';
import PageSection from '@src/components/pages/PageSection.astro';
import { routes } from '@src/data/routes';
import { site } from '@src/data/site';
import Base from '@src/layouts/Base.astro';
import { lastModified } from '@src/lib/dates';
import { routeSeo } from '@src/lib/seo/meta';

const updated = lastModified(...routes['/about'].sources);
---

<Base seo={routeSeo('/about')}>
  <PageHero
    kicker="about"
    title="Noticing when a person is doing a machine’s job."
    updated={updated}
  >
    <p>
      Most of what I have built started as somebody, usually me, doing the same thing by hand for
      the third time.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <PageSection id="story" title="The short version">
    <p>
      I was born in the United States, grew up in Pakistan, and came back at eighteen with a plan
      somebody else had made for me: start at a two-year school. Wilbur Wright College was the one I
      landed at, nowhere near where I lived, more or less by accident. A conversation in a physics
      class put me onto its engineering pathway, which guarantees a transfer if you hold your
      grades. Two and a half years later I went to the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign with
      a cohort of friends.
    </p>
    <p>
      Along the way I became the first computer science tutor the college had, teaching calculus,
      physics, chemistry and two semesters of C. That is how Dr. Espiritu found me, and how I ended
      up on her research into matching mentors with mentees: students who had already transferred,
      paired with students still trying to. The interesting part was deciding what a good pairing
      even is. Close enough to have something in common, different enough that one of them has
      something to teach.
    </p>
    <p>
      I paid for all of it out of pocket. Gas stations, busboy shifts, cleaning toilets, tutoring,
      lab assistant work, and a few hundred projects built in whatever was left of the evening.
    </p>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="work" title="What I do now" ground="body">
    <p>
      {site.jobTitle} at {site.employer.name}. I emailed their COO while I was still in school,
      explaining who I was and why I wanted to work there, and started three days after I finished.
      I moved to Austin for the tech sector and it has been the right call. The work is sitting with
      a client, finding the step a person is doing by hand, and building whatever removes it:
      sometimes product, sometimes the systems underneath.
      <a href="/experience">The experience page</a> has the roles in detail, and
      <a href="/#projects">the projects</a> are the work I set myself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Outside it I buy broken hardware and fix it. A lot I picked up last spring came with four
      Bambu Lab printers, which is how I ended up running a small printing business on Etsy without
      ever deciding to. Most of what that taught me was unglamorous: filament absorbs moisture and
      has to be dried, the wrong material fails in ways you only learn by failing, and a printer
      that jams at hour nine costs you the whole print. AMS-X came out of the most repetitive part
      of it. Four spools is a hardware answer to a software problem.
    </p>
    <p>I also watch an unreasonable amount of financial reality television.</p>
  </PageSection>
</Base>
